WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
When building accessible interfaces in React Native,

2
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.400
the primary challenge is mapping two distinct engineering environments

3
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.200
without losing semantic meaning in translation.

4
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.600
The framework avoids creating a bespoke mental model for accessibility.

5
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:20.800
Instead, it operates directly as a thin wrapper over iOS's native UI accessibility framework.

6
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.800
Everything you know from UIKit,

7
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.600
how voiceover reads the screen,

8
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.600
how dynamic type scales text,

9
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.000
and how reduced motion effects animations

10
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.600
remains applicable.

11
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.400
The only requirement is learning the deterministic API translation map

12
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.600
between Swift and JSX.

13
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.200
The main structural difference you will encounter is how state is handled.

14
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:45.000
While UIKit often combines static elements and dynamic states into the same property,

15
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.000
React Native separates them.

16
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.400
You will also navigate a behavioral shift.

17
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.800
React Native inverts the visibility defaults for container elements

18
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.000
compared to standard UI views.

19
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.200
Mastering React Native accessibility is a syntax translation exercise.

20
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.800
The system is designed to preserve native semantic meaning

21
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.800
by speaking a specific JSX dialect.

22
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:11.600
In UIKit, you assign static semantics to a view using the accessibility traits property.

23
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.200
React Native replaces this property with accessibility role,

24
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.400
accepting a single string value.

25
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.600
A dot button trait in Swift becomes button in JSX.

26
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.400
While UIKit appends dynamic conditions like dot selected to that trait array,

27
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.400
React Native requires them in a distinctly separate accessibility state object.

28
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:37.000
If you are building a custom control that lacks a direct one-to-one UI accessibility equivalent,

29
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.400
the fallback strategy is straightforward.

30
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.800
Assign the role of none and combine it with an explicit accessibility label

31
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.600
to provide voiceover with the necessary context.

32
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.000
This strict API separation of concerns prevents semantic pollution.

33
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.800
It keeps your static definitions clean and aligns accessibility with reacts data-driven state management.

34
00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.600
Beyond individual elements, you have to account for a structural divergence

35
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.600
regarding how iOS and React Native handle container views.

36
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:11.200
In UIKit, a standard UIV defaults to ignoring accessibility entirely.

37
00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.600
React Native inverts this baseline.

38
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.800
Applying the accessible prop to apparent container view

39
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.400
changes how the system parses its children.

40
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.800
When you set accessible equals true on that parent container,

41
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.800
that view and all of its children are treated as a single,

42
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.000
focusable element by voiceover.

43
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.400
Use this specific grouping pattern for list row cells or metadata rows.

44
00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.400
Leaving data fragments isolated destroys meaning.

45
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.000
Grouping them ensures voiceover reads the related text together.

46
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:45.000
Grouping elements is different from the requirement to completely remove a decorative element tree from voiceover's radar.

47
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.000
To replicate UIKit's cascading logic, where is accessibility element is set to false,

48
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.000
you use the important for accessibility prop and set it to no hide descendants.

49
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.400
Proactive manipulation of the accessibility tree prevents fragmented reading orders.

50
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:06.200
It guarantees you are restoring standard native user experience expectations for screen readers.

51
00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.000
Static trees are predictable, but asynchronous view updates,

52
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.400
like validation errors surfacing outside of standard navigation transitions,

53
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.400
create focus challenges.

54
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.800
UIKit relies on the UI accessibility.post notification system,

55
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:25.800
specifically the dot screen changed argument to alert voiceover to these shifts.

56
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.600
The React Native equivalent is the accessibility info API.

57
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:35.200
You use its announce for accessibility method to command the system to read new context allowed.

58
00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:39.800
To manually redirect voiceover focus to a specific element after an update,

59
00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:45.200
capture a reference to that element and pass it to accessibility info dot set accessibility focus.

60
00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:51.200
This imperative focus management is crucial when handling custom bottom sheets and UI overlays.

61
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:56.000
If you build a custom modal without using React Native's built-in component,

62
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.200
voiceover will read the hidden background content.

63
00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.200
You must apply the accessibility view is modal prop.

64
00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:09.600
This acts as an absolute barrier, telling voiceover to ignore everything outside that specific view.

65
00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:14.600
Mastering imperative focus management and modal properties is mandatory.

66
00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:19.800
Without them, screen reader users can find themselves trapped in inactive UI layers.

67
00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:25.600
Beyond component level traits, you must respect OS level user accessibility preferences,

68
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.200
starting with iOS dynamic type.

69
00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.600
While UIKit observes UI font dot preferred font,

70
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.800
React Native's text components scale by default through the allow font scaling prop.

71
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:40.600
To adapt custom layout dimensions for those larger text sizes,

72
00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:45.200
read the current scale multiplier using pixel ratio dot get font scaling.

73
00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.400
You must also handle the system level iOS reduce motion setting.

74
00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:55.000
Branch your code and suppress complex animation logic based on this specific OS preference.

75
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.400
If you are using the reanimated three library,

76
00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.400
you execute this branch by implementing the use reduced motion hook.

77
00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:07.200
To monitor for runtime setting changes while the app is active,

78
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.200
observe accessibility info dot add event listener.

79
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:15.800
Intercepting and respecting the system level scaling and motion preferences

80
00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:19.600
is a requirement for passing native accessibility audits.

81
00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.600
Once your mappings are implemented,

82
00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:26.400
you have to verify that the accessibility tree actually behaves as expected.

83
00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.400
Relying solely on the iOS simulator to test voiceover behavior

84
00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:33.200
introduces significant limitations.

85
00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:38.600
The simulator lacks swipe gestures, haptic feedback and accurate audio routing.

86
00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:42.800
Critically, the timing behaviors around how voiceover focus changes

87
00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:46.000
differ from the native experience on actual hardware.

88
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:51.200
Firm, limit the simulator to pairing with the Xcode accessibility inspector.

89
00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.800
This setup is efficient for static verification of your tree structure, labels and roles.

90
00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:00.400
True behavioral validation requires a physical iPhone.

91
00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:03.800
On the device, swipe right through every interactive element

92
00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:08.000
to ensure the sequential swipe order matches the visual layout order.

93
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.200
Next, run the interactive checks.

94
00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.200
Verify that double tap actions fire correctly

95
00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:17.600
and use swipe gestures to confirm value changes on adjustable controls.

96
00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:19.800
Finally, perform boundary checks.

97
00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:23.400
Navigate into custom models to confirm focus is trapped

98
00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:27.600
and ensure it correctly restores to the underlying screen upon dismissal.

99
00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.200
Because ReactMative acts as a thin wrapper,

100
00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:34.600
its accessibility success depends on preserving native iOS semantics.

101
00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.600
This can only be proven through native physical testing.

